{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>חלוץ</b> — twice in the Masorah: here and the other חלוץ הנעל (the shoe). And this is what we say: everyone who goes out to the war of the House of David writes a bill of divorce for his wife, so that she not be claimed by the yavam and not require חליצה from the yavam. And we hear from here that Moses too did so, and from him David learned. And this is: 'and every חלוץ shall cross over for you' — חלוץ הנעל (the shoe)."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The verse quoted, 'ועבר לכם כל חלוץ' (Numbers 32:21), is read as a double allusion: the plain sense refers to armed men (חלוץ = one girt for battle), but the juxtaposition with חלוץ הנעל (Deuteronomy 25:9, the shoe-removal in levirate redemption) yields the homiletic link to David's practice. The phrase 'ואידך חלוץ הנעל' identifies the second Masorah occurrence without a verse address, as written."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:23:39.038225442Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}